/** \addtogroup area_distributed */
/** \defgroup theory_distributed Theory of distributed algorithms
 \ingroup area_distributed

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/distributed` realize, beyond the book,
 the algorithms whose data are spread over the processes of MPI, each with a
 memory of its own, which exchange them only by messages; the area holds the
 sort by regular sampling of `DistributedSort.h`, whose processes may also
 run on the threads of an executor (see \ref theory_parallel), and the
 runtime of the processes as workers: their executor, their mailbox with
 the detection of the termination, and the minimum of a value over them
 (`MPIExecutor.h` and `MPIMailbox.h`). This page collects what the headers
 take for granted: the model of the cost of the messages and its notation
 (see \ref theory_distributed_notation), the property on which the sort
 rests, with the argument behind its bound (see
 \ref theory_distributed_sort), and the detection of the termination by the
 four counters of Mattern (see \ref theory_distributed_messages). The
 pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the paper that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_distributed_notation Definitions and notation

 A distributed computation runs on \f$ P \f$ processes, numbered by their
 <em>ranks</em> \f$ 0 , \ldots , P - 1 \f$ in a communicator, each with its
 own memory and its own part of the input; a process reads the data of
 another only by receiving a message. A <em>collective</em> operation is one
 that all the processes of the communicator call, each with its part, e.g.,
 the gathering of a value of each process in all of them
 (`MPI_Allgather`) or the exchange in which each process sends a piece of its
 data to every other one (`MPI_Alltoall`, and `MPI_Alltoallv` for pieces of
 different sizes). The cost of a message of \f$ b \f$ bytes is taken as
 \f$ \alpha + \beta b \f$, \f$ \alpha \f$ the latency and \f$ \beta \f$ the
 time per byte, and the cost of an algorithm is its local work, its number of
 messages and its volume of communication, all per process, i.e., along its
 slowest process.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ P \f$</td>
     <td>the processes of the communicator <tt>comm</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n_i \f$, \f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>the items of the process \f$ i \f$, and \f$ n = \sum_i n_i \f$ the
         items of all</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha \f$, \f$ \beta \f$</td>
     <td>the latency of a message and the time per byte</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s_1 , \ldots , s_{P - 1} \f$</td>
     <td>the splitters of the sort, which cut the keys into \f$ P \f$
         intervals, one per process</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ w \f$</td>
     <td>the items of a process between two of its samples,
         \f$ n_i / P \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s_i \f$, \f$ r_i \f$</td>
     <td>the messages sent and received by the process \f$ i \f$ in a
         computation of the mailbox</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ S_k \f$, \f$ R_k \f$</td>
     <td>the sums of \f$ s_i \f$ and of \f$ r_i \f$ read by the wave
         \f$ k \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 A sort of the items spread over the processes leaves in the process of rank
 \f$ j \f$ a sorted vector, whose items are not smaller than those of the
 ranks before \f$ j \f$, so that the vectors in the order of the ranks are the
 sorted items of all. Every distributed sort moves most items to another
 process, since a process holds, before the sort, items of every interval:
 the volume of communication is at least about \f$ n / P \f$ per process, and
 the aim is to send each item once, in one exchange, with parts of about
 \f$ n / P \f$ items at the end.

 \section theory_distributed_sort Sorting by regular sampling

 The sort of Shi and Schaeffer (`sort()` of `DistributedSort.h`) sorts the
 items of each process, takes from them \f$ P - 1 \f$ samples at the ranks
 \f$ \lfloor ( j + 1 ) n_i / P \rfloor \f$, i.e., one every \f$ w \f$ items,
 gathers all the \f$ P ( P - 1 ) \f$ samples in every process and sorts them,
 and takes as splitters every \f$ ( P - 1 ) \f$-th of them. Each process then
 cuts its sorted items at the splitters by binary searches, sends to the
 process \f$ j \f$ its items in \f$ ( s_j , s_{j+1} ] \f$, and merges the
 \f$ P \f$ sorted pieces that it receives. Since every process cuts at the
 same splitters, the items received by the process \f$ j \f$ are exactly those
 of all the processes in its interval, and their merge is sorted; hence the
 vectors in the order of the ranks are the sorted items.

 <em>Lemma 1 (regular sampling).</em> With \f$ n / P \f$ items in each process
 and distinct keys, each process receives
 \f[
   \text{fewer than } 2 n / P \text{ items.} \tag{1}
 \f]

 Indeed, the interval of a process holds \f$ P - 1 \f$ samples, those between
 two consecutive splitters, and let \f$ t_i \f$ be those of the process
 \f$ i \f$, so that \f$ \sum_i t_i \le P - 1 \f$. The items of the process
 \f$ i \f$ in the interval lie between its sample before the first of its
 \f$ t_i \f$ samples and its sample after the last one, i.e., in at most
 \f$ t_i + 1 \f$ of its gaps of \f$ w = n / P^2 \f$ items; hence the interval
 holds at most
 \f$ \sum_i ( t_i + 1 ) w \le ( 2 P - 1 ) n / P^2 < 2 n / P \f$ items, which
 is (1). The bound is tight up to a constant, and in practice the parts are
 far closer to \f$ n / P \f$. With repeated keys (1) may fail, since all the
 items of a key go to one interval; `sort()` therefore cuts the items equal to
 a run of \f$ k \f$ equal splitters into \f$ k + 1 \f$ parts of the same size,
 so that a key repeated many times is spread over as many processes as it
 fills, and the merge keeps the result sorted since the parts are of equal
 items.

 The cost of the sort is the local sort, \f$ O( ( n / P ) \log( n / P ) ) \f$,
 the sort of the samples, \f$ O( P^2 \log P ) \f$, the \f$ P - 1 \f$ binary
 searches, and the merge of \f$ P \f$ pieces of \f$ O( n / P ) \f$ items in
 all, \f$ O( ( n / P ) \log P ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( ( n / P ) \log n ) \f$ work
 per process when \f$ n / P \ge P^2 \f$. The communication is three
 collective operations: the gathering of the samples, \f$ P^2 \f$ items to
 each process; the exchange of the sizes of the pieces, one integer for each
 pair of processes; and the exchange of the pieces, each item sent once,
 which by (1) costs each process \f$ O( n / P ) \f$ items. With a direct
 all-to-all, in which each process sends \f$ P - 1 \f$ messages, the time of
 the communication is therefore
 \f[
   O( \alpha P + \beta ( n / P + P^2 ) ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 which is \f$ O( \alpha P + \beta n / P ) \f$ when \f$ n / P \ge P^2 \f$.
 Beyond some thousands of processes the latency \f$ \alpha P \f$ and the
 \f$ P^2 \f$ samples dominate (2); the multi-level sorts of Axtmann, Bingmann,
 Sanders and Schulz then group the processes and exchange the items in
 \f$ O( \log P ) \f$ levels, each item sent once per level, which trades the
 \f$ P \f$ messages of each process for \f$ O( P^{1 / \ell} ) \f$ messages per
 level over \f$ \ell \f$ levels.

 \section theory_distributed_messages Messages, owners and termination

 The processes of a communicator are the workers of a distributed
 computation (`MPIExecutor` of `MPIExecutor.h`, whose `run( f )` makes the
 call `f( rank )` in each process): each owns a part of the data, the items
 whose keys hash to its rank (`ownerOf( k , P )` of `Mailbox.h`, the hash of
 \f$ k \f$ modulo \f$ P \f$), and sends to the owner what concerns them, e.g.,
 the nodes of a search that the other processes generate. The mailbox of
 `MPIMailbox.h` gathers the messages of a process for each rank into an
 outbox and sends it as one message of MPI when it holds a batch, by
 `MPI_Isend`, which does not block: the buffer stays in a list of the sends
 in flight until MPI has completed it, which `MPI_Testsome` finds at each
 poll, so that a process never waits for a receiver; the batches that have
 come are found by `MPI_Iprobe` from any source and received by `MPI_Recv`.
 The batches trade the latency \f$ \alpha \f$ of a message for its volume:
 \f$ k \f$ messages of \f$ b \f$ bytes cost \f$ \alpha + \beta k b \f$
 instead of \f$ k ( \alpha + \beta b ) \f$, and the latency of a single
 message grows by the time its batch takes to fill, which `flush()` cuts.
 This is the asynchronous mailbox of the library YGM of Priest, Steil,
 Sanders and Pearce; the exchange of NBX of Hoefler, Siebert and Lumsdaine
 solves instead a single round of sparse messages, by synchronous sends and
 a barrier that does not block.

 A batch is an array of messages when they are trivially copyable, which
 MPI then copies as bytes (several kinds of them travel as one
 `std::variant`, itself trivially copyable). Messages of other kinds, or of
 lengths that vary (the nodes of a search with a state of variable size),
 are records of `MPIByteMailbox`: each a tag of 32 bits, a length and the
 bytes, padded to 8, packed into a batch until it holds `batch` bytes, and
 `MPIMailbox` of a type that is not trivially copyable is such a mailbox
 whose bytes are those of `MPICodec`, the customization point that encodes
 and decodes a message. Whatever their kind, the messages are counted one by
 one, so that the waves below see all of them.

 A process is <em>active</em> while it has work and <em>passive</em>
 otherwise, becomes active only by receiving, and sends only while active;
 the computation has terminated when all the processes are passive and no
 message is on its way. Each process counts the messages it has sent,
 \f$ s_i \f$, and those it has received, \f$ r_i \f$, both of which only
 grow. A <em>wave</em> reads the counters of all the processes and sums
 them, \f$ S = \sum_i s_i \f$ and \f$ R = \sum_i r_i \f$; here it is a
 reduction that does not block (`MPI_Iallreduce`), to which each process
 contributes the pair \f$ ( s_i , r_i ) \f$ copied when it is passive, i.e.,
 from `quiescent()`, while it keeps receiving and working until the wave
 completes, and a process starts a wave only after the previous one has
 completed in it, which happens only after all the processes have
 contributed to it.

 <em>Lemma 2 (Mattern's four counters).</em> Let the wave \f$ k - 1 \f$
 read the receipts \f$ R_{k - 1} \f$ and the next wave \f$ k \f$ the sends
 \f$ S_k \f$. If
 \f[
   S_k = R_{k - 1} , \tag{3}
 \f]
 the computation had terminated when the last process contributed to the
 wave \f$ k - 1 \f$.

 Indeed, let \f$ T \f$ be the moment of the last contribution to the wave
 \f$ k - 1 \f$, which precedes every contribution to the wave \f$ k \f$, and
 \f$ S( T ) \f$, \f$ R( T ) \f$ the messages sent and received by all at
 \f$ T \f$. The counters only grow, the receipts of the wave \f$ k - 1 \f$
 were read at or before \f$ T \f$ and the sends of the wave \f$ k \f$ at or
 after it, and a message is received after it is sent; hence
 \f[
   R_{k - 1} \le R( T ) \le S( T ) \le S_k ,
 \f]
 and (3) makes them all equal. Thus at \f$ T \f$ no message was on its way
 (\f$ R( T ) = S( T ) \f$), and no process had received a message after it
 contributed to the wave \f$ k - 1 \f$ (\f$ R_{k - 1} = R( T ) \f$): each
 process was passive when it contributed, and a passive process becomes
 active only by receiving, so that all were passive at \f$ T \f$, which is
 the termination. Conversely, once the computation has terminated, every
 process eventually finds no work and contributes to every wave, the
 counters stop growing, and two waves after the termination
 \f$ S_k = R_{k - 1} \f$ holds; since every process gets the same sums, all
 the processes stop at the same wave. A wave costs a reduction of two
 integers, \f$ O( \alpha \log P ) \f$ time, so that the termination is known
 \f$ O( \alpha \log P ) \f$ after it happens, with no message per message
 sent (the acknowledgments of Dijkstra and Scholten) and no process that
 coordinates the waves.

 A process may also stop the whole computation before its termination
 (a limit on the work, a bound that closes the search), by `stop()`: it
 sends a <em>note</em>, an empty batch, to each other process, and from
 then on posts and receives nothing; a process whose poll finds a note
 stops in the same way, and leaves its work for `quiescent()`. A stopped
 process contributes to the waves the triple \f$ ( s_i , r_i , 1 ) \f$, the
 others \f$ ( s_i , r_i , 0 ) \f$, and the third sum \f$ \Sigma \f$ of a wave
 counts the processes stopped when they contributed to it.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the stop).</em> If a process calls `stop()`, every process
 sees the end at the same wave, at most two waves after the last one has
 received the note; if \f$ \Sigma_k = P \f$ for the wave \f$ k \f$, all the
 processes had stopped when they contributed to it.

 Indeed, the notes are messages of MPI, which delivers them; a process that
 does not stop by itself polls while it works, or in `quiescent()` while it
 waits, and stops when it receives its note. Once stopped, a process stays
 stopped, and since it receives nothing more its work does not grow, so
 that it calls `quiescent()` until the end; the first wave started by all
 after the last of them has stopped has \f$ \Sigma = P \f$, and every
 process, which has the same sums, sees the end at that wave. The waves are
 started by each process only after the previous one has completed, so that
 they are the same collective operations in the same order on all the
 processes, also when the end comes from (3) instead of from
 \f$ \Sigma = P \f$: a computation whose processes have all stopped can also
 meet (3), if nothing is on its way, and it is then over in both senses.

 After the end, `leftovers( f )` hands to \f$ f \f$ the messages that no poll
 has handed: those still on their way to the process, those to itself and
 those of its outboxes. The first are known by counting: each process keeps
 the messages \f$ m_{i , q} \f$ and the notes \f$ n_{i , q} \f$ that it has
 sent by MPI to each process \f$ q \f$, and one reduction that scatters its
 sums (`MPI_Reduce_scatter_block`) gives each process \f$ q \f$ the totals
 \f[
   M_q = \sum_i m_{i , q} , \qquad N_q = \sum_i n_{i , q} \tag{4}
 \f]
 of what was sent to it; the process then receives, by blocking calls,
 until it has received \f$ M_q \f$ messages and \f$ N_q \f$ notes in all, and
 waits for its own sends, which its receivers are receiving in the same
 way.

 <em>Lemma 4 (each message once).</em> After `leftovers()` on every
 process, each message given to `send()` on any process has been handed to
 a function exactly once, by a `poll()` or by `leftovers()`.

 Indeed, a message given to `send()` is in an outbox until its batch is
 posted, then either among the messages of the process to itself or on its
 way by MPI, and finally handed by the poll that receives it. A stopped
 process posts nothing, so that what it holds in its outboxes or for itself
 is handed by its `leftovers()`; what was posted by MPI to \f$ q \f$ is
 counted in \f$ M_q \f$ of (4), and \f$ q \f$ receives until it has
 received \f$ M_q \f$ messages, those of its polls included, which are
 exactly those of the batches posted to it, since MPI delivers each message
 once. The cost is one reduction of \f$ 2 P \f$ integers and the messages
 left, i.e., \f$ O( \alpha \log P + \beta P ) \f$ time besides them, paid
 once at the end, and nothing during the computation but a load of a flag
 per send; the destructor makes the same exchange, dropping the messages,
 if `leftovers()` was not called, so that no send stays in flight.

 The bound of a search that every process improves (the value of the best
 solution found so far) is shared the same way: `DistributedMin` starts a
 reduction of the minimum that does not block and tests it while the process
 works, so that a process learns the bounds of the others at most one
 reduction late, without a message for each improvement.

 The sort of \ref theory_distributed_sort runs on the processes themselves
 (the communicator of an `MPIExecutor`, `comm()`), each with the threads of
 an executor of its own inside: no part of it simulates the processes.

 \section theory_distributed_refs References

 \see H. Shi, J. Schaeffer, Parallel sorting by regular sampling, Journal
      of Parallel and Distributed Computing 14(4), 361-372, 1992,
      doi:10.1016/0743-7315(92)90075-X
 \see M. Axtmann, T. Bingmann, P. Sanders, C. Schulz, Practical massively
      parallel sorting, ACM Symposium on Parallelism in Algorithms and
      Architectures (SPAA), 13-23, 2015, doi:10.1145/2755573.2755595
 \see E. W. Dijkstra, C. S. Scholten, Termination detection for diffusing
      computations, Information Processing Letters 11(1), 1-4, 1980,
      doi:10.1016/0020-0190(80)90021-6
 \see F. Mattern, Algorithms for distributed termination detection,
      Distributed Computing 2(3), 161-175, 1987, doi:10.1007/BF01782776
 \see T. Hoefler, A. Lumsdaine, W. Rehm, Implementation and performance
      analysis of non-blocking collective operations for MPI, ACM/IEEE
      Conference on Supercomputing (SC), 1-10, 2007,
      doi:10.1145/1362622.1362692
 \see T. Hoefler, C. Siebert, A. Lumsdaine, Scalable communication
      protocols for dynamic sparse data exchange, ACM SIGPLAN Symposium on
      Principles and Practice of Parallel Programming (PPoPP), 159-168,
      2010, doi:10.1145/1693453.1693476
 \see B. Priest, T. Steil, G. Sanders, R. Pearce, You've Got Mail (YGM):
      building missing asynchronous communication primitives, IEEE
      International Parallel and Distributed Processing Symposium
      Workshops (IPDPSW), 221-230, 2019, doi:10.1109/IPDPSW.2019.00045
